"""
The first board's last layer, with a range that clips nothing.

pta_board_chain.py ran a stand-in for the GRXGPU charter's first-board
classifier with its last layer on the tile, and found the largest error
decided, in five networks of thirty, by the converter's range and not by
noise.  grx930's harness sets a converter's range on a thousand training
images, to pass all but one sum in ten thousand.  That is a rule for the
class, which a clipped sum seldom moves.  The charter's demonstration holds
the layer's raw outputs to a reference and says to "define full scale from
the frozen golden manifest".  This is that: the last layer's range fitted to
its declared reference on the images that are run.

    fitted         the smallest step at which the largest the declared
                   reference is, is within the converter's full scale, 127
                   codes
    a bit spare    the smallest at which it is within half of it: one bit of
                   headroom over fitted

WHAT A RANGE COSTS.  B14's noise is in LSB of the converter: 0.25 of an LSB
at the receiver, 30 photons an LSB.  A converter's range is hardware, and a
sum is fitted to it by turning the light or the gain down.  So with a larger
step the receiver's noise and the rounding are larger in a sum's own units,
by as much, and the shot noise by the root of it; the programming error and
the crosstalk, which go with the sum, are what they were.  That is how
grx930's harness has it: a fitted converter's noise is in its own LSB.

MEASURED IN A MODEL, by grx930 (c930/doc/pta_error_model_design_note.md section
5, "The first board's last layer, with a range that clips nothing",
2026-10-13; `SHAPE=chain sim/pta_mnist.sh DIR WORK chainfit`, and `pta_mnist
eval --gatefit F`).  pta_board_chain.py's networks, tile and draws, and its
format alone and its tile as budgeted, each three ways: with the range
training's rule gives, which are that file's own runs, line for line; fitted;
and with a bit spare.  On 10,000 images and on the first thousand.  A fitted
row is read over its own row with training's range, and a row with a bit
spare over the fitted one, draw for draw.

DERIVED here:

  a row's mean and  which have to be the seven tables grx930 printed, cell
  its error         for cell.  A row with training's range is worked out from
                    pta_board_chain.py's figures, which this file does not
                    hold again
  what a row adds   how often right in the row it is read over, less in the
                    row, network by network
  what a bit costs  a network's rms error with a larger step over its rms
                    error with the smaller, draw for draw: the two are
                    against one reference, so it is a ratio of errors in the
                    sum's own units
  in steps, and of  as pta_board_chain.py has them, at the row's own step
  full scale

HOW SURE.  Five networks, 2.8 errors for one in twenty.  A fitted run whose
step did not move is its run with training's range, figure for figure, so
what fitting adds is the networks that moved and nothing else: five of
thirty on 10,000 images, two on the first thousand.  A bit spare moves all
thirty.

WHAT THIS IS NOT.  grxgpu's classifier, or a chip: everything
pta_board_chain.py says of itself.  A range a board could set without its
golden manifest: this one is fitted to the very images it is then run on.  A
converter with more bits, which is the other way to buy range.  A lit tile.

Standard library only.  Run:  python3 docs/designs/pta_board_range.py
"""
